# Windows CI checked the tree out with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrote every
# .go file's LF into CRLF on disk. gofmt treats a carriage return as
# non-canonical formatting, so the gofmt step failed listing essentially every
# Go file in the repository -- a line-ending artifact reported as a formatting
# error. The blobs were LF in the repository the whole time.
#
# `text eol=lf` pins the working-tree ending regardless of the runner's
# autocrlf setting, so the bytes gofmt sees on Windows are the bytes that were
# committed. Go source is LF by definition -- gofmt emits it and will not
# accept anything else -- so this records a fact rather than imposing a style.
*.go text eol=lf

# The same reasoning for the rest of what the gate reads. A shell script with
# CRLF fails on a Unix runner at the shebang, and go.mod/go.sum are compared
# byte-for-byte by the toolchain.
*.sh  text eol=lf
*.mod text eol=lf
*.sum text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.md  text eol=lf

# Everything else: let Git detect text and leave the ending alone. testdata/ is
# not tracked here, but XML fixtures are a case where a CRLF can be the thing
# under test -- an XML parser must normalise it per the Recommendation -- so
# nothing in this file may rewrite one.
* text=auto

# c14n's golden files are canonical XML, compared byte for byte: a canonical
# form is LF by definition, and a CR in one is written as &#xD;. Under
# `* text=auto` above, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true would rewrite
# their LF to CRLF and every golden comparison would fail on a line-ending
# artifact. -text keeps Git's hands off the bytes. It comes after `* text=auto`
# because the last matching line wins.
tests/c14n/testdata/** -text

# The c14n fetch manifest is read line by line by tests/fetch-c14n.sh; a CRLF
# checkout would leave a carriage return on every URL and digest.
tests/c14n-corpus.txt text eol=lf
